//? Title: Lenstra–Lenstra–Lovasz lattice reduction
//? Subtitle: Actual row operations, original skew basis and exact integer transform witnesses
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
LLL alternates integer size reduction with a floating Gram-Schmidt inequality
that may trigger row swaps. We retain the original skewed3-by-3 basis, expose
actual operations and an exact checked integer transform, and separate lattice
preservation from computed reduction quality. Equal Gram determinants alone do
not prove two bases generate the same lattice, and floating completion does not
certify an exact shortest-vector theorem.
)

\toc

> Integer operations and floating decisions

Write each basis row as b_i=b_i^*+sum_{j<i}mu_ij*b_j^*. Size reduction subtracts
q*b_j from b_i, with q the rounded computed mu_ij when its magnitude exceeds one
half. The adjacent Lovasz test compares

$$\|b_k^*\|^2\ge(\delta-\mu_{k,k-1}^2)\|b_{k-1}^*\|^2,\qquad\tfrac14<\delta<1.$$
\alttext(The computed adjacent orthogonal norm must exceed the delta-adjusted predecessor norm; a failed test swaps adjacent basis rows.)

A size-reduction row subtraction with integer q is unimodular, and a row swap is
unimodular. Starting U at the identity and applying exactly the same operations
to U proves B_current=U*B_original with determinant(U)=plus or minus1. This is
the lattice-preservation reason. The implementation checks every exact
basis/transform coordinate in widened integer arithmetic before committing a
complete row, and throws if signed64 storage cannot hold it.

By contrast, the decisions use double Gram-Schmidt and rounded projections. The
exact-arithmetic LLL quality/termination theorems do not automatically become
rigorous certificates for finite-precision decisions. An explicit outer-step
budget bounds the run; completion status and observed computed inequalities are
reported separately. Neither row identity nor each individual row norm is
promised to decrease.

> Tiny actual size reduction

For rows(1,0),(3,1), the actual first projection is3. The producing integer
operation b1←b1−3*b0 gives(0,1), and the transform’s second row becomes(−3,1).
The next actual Lovasz test passes at delta0.75. All displayed values below come
from the native operation trace.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lll_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/lll.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t = dm::algorithms::lll_trace({{1, 0}, {3, 1}});
    auto e = t.events.front();
    std::print("| actual event kind0:reduce,1:Lovasz,2:swap | actual target row | actual source row | " "actual integer multiplier | actual pre-operation projection | actual left | actual right | " "actual check passed |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
    for (auto &r : t.events)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", int(r.kind),
                   r.k, r.j, r.multiplier, r.projection, r.left, r.right,
                   r.passed);
    auto &state = t.states[e.state];
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("old", {0, 0},
           std::format("Actual original target row\n({}, {})", t.original[1][0],
                       t.original[1][1])).node("operation", {6, 0},
                                               std::format(
                                               "Actual integer operation\nrow{} minus{} times row{}",
                                               e.k, e.multiplier, e.j)).node(
        "new", {12, 0},
        std::format("Actual committed target\n({}, {})", state.basis[1][0],
                    state.basis[1][1])).edge("old", "operation").edge(
        "operation", "new");
    d.save_svg("build/lll_tiny.svg");
}
```
